**user**:
> Can I make some labels bold in ggplot?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can make labels bold in ggplot by using the `element_text()` function within the `theme()` function, specifying the `face` argument as `"bold"`. You can make various text elements bold, such as axis titles, axis text, plot title, legend text, etc. Here's an example of how to do this:
> 
> ```r
> library(ggplot2)
> 
> # Sample data
> data <- data.frame(
>   x = c("A", "B", "C"),
>   y = c(2, 4, 3)
> )
> 
> # Basic ggplot
> p <- ggplot(data, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
>   geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
>   ggtitle("Bold Title Example") +
>   xlab("Bold X-axis Label") +
>   ylab("Bold Y-axis Label")
> 
> # Make specific labels bold
> p + theme(
>   plot.title = element_text(face = "bold"),      # Bold plot title
>   axis.title.x = element_text(face = "bold"),    # Bold x-axis title
>   axis.title.y = element_text(face = "bold"),    # Bold y-axis title
>   legend.title = element_text(face = "bold"),    # Bold legend title (if applicable)
>   legend.text = element_text(face = "bold")      # Bold legend text (if applicable)
> )
> ```
> 
> In this example, the plot title, x-axis label, and y-axis label are made bold. You can adjust the `face = "bold"` setting for other text elements similarly if needed.
**user**:
> I only want some labels bold. Like, I have a categorical axis, and I want a few of the entries that axis bold and the rest not.
**assistant**:
> To selectively bold specific labels on a categorical axis in ggplot, you can use the `scale_x_discrete()` function alongside the `element_markdown()` function from the `ggtext` package, which allows for rich text formatting. Here’s how you can do it:
> 
> 1. Install and load the `ggtext` package if you haven’t already.
> 2. Use markdown syntax to format specific labels as bold.
> 
> Here's an example:
> 
> ```r
> # Install ggtext package if not already installed
> # install.packages("ggtext")
> 
> library(ggplot2)
> library(ggtext)  # Load the ggtext package
> 
> # Sample data
> data <- data.frame(
>   x = c("A", "B", "C", "D"),
>   y = c(2, 4, 3, 5)
> )
> 
> # Create a ggplot with selective bold formatting
> p <- ggplot(data, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
>   geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
>   ylab("Value") +
>   ggtitle("Selective Bold Labels on Categorical Axis")
> 
> # Define which labels to format as bold
> bold_labels <- c("A", "C")
> 
> # Use scale_x_discrete with element_markdown
> p + scale_x_discrete(labels = function(x) {
>     ifelse(x %in% bold_labels, paste0("**", x, "**"), x)
> }) +
>   theme(
>     axis.text.x = element_markdown()  # Enable markdown rendering for x-axis text
>   )
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> - **`bold_labels`**: A vector containing the labels you want to be bold.
> - **`scale_x_discrete(labels = function(x) { ... })`**: This function customizes the x-axis labels. If the label is in `bold_labels`, it wraps it in markdown syntax to make it bold.
> - **`element_markdown()`**: This allows the x-axis text to interpret markdown syntax.
> 
> With this setup, labels "A" and "C" will be displayed in bold on the categorical axis while the others will remain as regular text.
